'' A UDT-returning call lands in a hidden temporary, and fbc destroys that temporary at
'' the END OF THE STATEMENT -- measured: 'MAKE( &TMP ); fb_PrintString( ..., &TMP, 1 );
'' _ZN1SD1Ev( &TMP )'. A loop head releases its own before branching, because the head
'' runs again on every iteration and the slot is the same one.
''
'' 'return t' out of a UDT function is a COPY CONSTRUCTION of the caller's storage --
'' '_ZN1SC1ERKS_( tmp$4$1, &T$1 )' followed by T's own destructor -- and not a
'' whole-aggregate store, which handed the caller a descriptor over released memory.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/udt-temp-life.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' GOLDEN: expected/udt-temp-life.out
